WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
<v Mike>The Hunt for Red October.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:03.940
<v Mike>Join the Hunt.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:27.180
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.840
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.120
<v Hosts>And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:39.760
<v Mike>The Hunt for Red October, a very serious film, maybe one of the most serious films we've covered in video game 8-bit form since Platoon.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.640
<v Mike>Yeah, you know, like, I feel like some of these are weird when they get video games and The Hunt for Red October is no exception.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:57.740
<v Mike>It starts very serious even on the video game with a wall of text and I just know every kid in America hits start as soon as that stuff started coming up on screen.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:04.920
<v Joe>Especially because it's like, it's all like American history and I feel like, you know, if you're like eight years old, you're like, okay, I'm not reading that.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.160
<v Sean>It very much is not American history.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.440
<v Sean>It's just the plot of the movie.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:15.460
<v Joe>Yeah, but I mean, November 12th, 1984, approximately four months before, you know, the Soviet Union and...

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.900
<v Sean>Come on. No, it's like serious.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.300
<v Joe>It's serious. It means video games are serious.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.440
<v Sean>It means that you're important for playing it.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.220
<v Joe>Come on. Look, I'm not saying I don't like it now.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.400
<v Joe>I'm just saying that I feel like a lot of kids, they're skipping through that.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
<v Sean>I think you're thinking of the current generation.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.920
<v Mike>I'm thinking of me when I was eight.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.020
<v Mike>Oh, okay. Yeah, 1984 to me would have sounded forever ago, even in 1991.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:50.460
<v Mike>Probably. Now, Sean, you are repeating some government lies there about this just being part of the movie.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:02:01.240
<v Mike>I want to remind you that it's repeated statements by both the Soviet and American governments is that nothing that we are about to see in this video game ever happened, all caps.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.060
<v Mike>So are you just perpetuating that myth here on the podcast as well?

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.300
<v Mike>Because I'm inclined to believe that at least some of this happened.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.520
<v Sean>Well, if we pull up Jack Ryan on the phone, maybe he could give us a bit more info.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.240
<v Joe>Yeah, so is this our first Tom Clancy game?

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.840
<v Joe>Like, is this like the beginning of the Tom Clancy games?

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.060
<v Mike>And it makes sense because this is also his first novel.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:24.820
<v Joe>See, because the...

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.360
<v Sean>We're getting Ghost Recon soon.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.680
<v Joe>The back of the box makes me feel like it's going to be...

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.840
<v Joe>Join the hunt makes me feel like it's going to be like a Pokemon Go, like, ARG style.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.820
<v Joe>Like, everyone is out there hunting for the Red October on their phone.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.320
<v Mike>In submarines, do you have to be in water for this?

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.320
<v Sean>Yes. It's like the Pokemon Go won't be in the water instead of land.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.960
<v Mike>But you can still give it a shot.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:54.840
<v Mike>True. Now, Tom Clancy, notable for the video game section, sure, but probably more prolific as a writer.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.520
<v Mike>I'm interested in both sides because I never really played...

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.320
<v Mike>There are tons of Tom Clancy's Splinter Cell, Tom Clancy's Ghost Recon.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.260
<v Mike>There's plenty of those games. I never really played most of any of that.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.600
<v Mike>So I'd be interested both from like a video game and literature perspective.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.800
<v Mike>What's your exposure to the Tom Clancy-verse?

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.740
<v Joe>Almost nothing other than like knowing what these video games are and knowing they're based on books.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.840
<v Sean>Like, that's it.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.080
<v Mike>Okay, so Sean's going to have to carry this segment.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.140
<v Joe>Please, this episode.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.460
<v Mike>Well, just this segment, just about Tom Clancy.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.560
<v Mike>You played the game, right?

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.760
<v Sean>No. You didn't play this game?

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.640
<v Joe>I haven't played one of these games in months.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.580
<v Joe>You guys just haven't noticed.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.320
<v Mike>You're doing a great job or we're doing a really bad job.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:47.080
<v Sean>Yeah. Well, yeah, I've played some Tom Clancy stuff.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.960
<v Sean>I've seen some Tom Clancy movies.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:56.320
<v Sean>I haven't read a Tom Clancy book, but I'd never say never.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.520
<v Sean>But yeah, Splinter Cell was great.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.740
<v Joe>I played some Rainbow Six Siege for a little bit when that was new.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:11.140
<v Sean>I played some Rainbow Six when it wasn't a games-of-service thing.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.680
<v Sean>Yeah, I've done some Tom Clancy's.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.840
<v Mike>But it's not always, is the focus on stealth?

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.740
<v Sean>No, the focus is on military.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.420
<v Mike>Right, so Splinter Cell is like the stealth adjacent but still military category?

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:36.280
<v Sean>I actually don't know if it's based on anything or if he just sort of workshopped the idea with them and then allowed them.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.740
<v Sean>Or if he just let them put his name on it.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.120
<v Mike>Right, which could very well happen.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.820
<v Sean>Yeah, but no, he's not a stealth writer.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:46.980
<v Sean>He's a military writer.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:53.980
<v Mike>Right. Yeah, I guess I always assumed that Splinter Cell was the answer to Metal Gear.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.660
<v Sean>I guess in a way, yeah.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.280
<v Mike>And in terms of the books, not really.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.040
<v Mike>But I mean, Tom Clancy's had many things adapted into movies as well.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.640
<v Mike>He's kind of like the spy military thriller version of Stephen King.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:21.840
<v Sean>Yeah, or the non-horror Dean Koontz is actually, I think, a better thing there for me.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.840
<v Sean>I think it talks more about the quality.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:34.260
<v Mike>The quality. And then how about the actual movie, The Hunt for Red October, with Sean Connery and Alec Baldwin?

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.940
<v Mike>Any watchers of that?

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.540
<v Sean>I read the back flap.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:39.300
<v Sean>I watched the trailer.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.540
<v Sean>I watched the movie clips, selection of nine scenes.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.460
<v Sean>I probably saw the movie when I was much younger, but I don't remember much of it now.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:55.380
<v Joe>I feel like I have a vague memory of maybe having watched it with my dad or something, but I don't remember it at all, if I did.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.700
<v Sean>The Hunt for Red October. Yes.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:04.820
<v Joe>I know that you got the classic Sean Connery voice in there, but that's all I know about it.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.760
<v Mike>And he's doing a good job of what the character is.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.640
<v Mike>The character, it's unknown his motives, right?

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.400
<v Mike>Is he going to attack the U.S.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.960
<v Mike>or is he defecting to the U.S.?

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:26.400
<v Mike>And I think Sean Connery plays the character in a way where you could rag on him for just being Sean Connery,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:34.460
<v Mike>but you could also say that he's doing this really good, riding the cusp of absurdity on his character as well.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.580
<v Mike>And I enjoyed his performance in this film.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:42.400
<v Mike>I also enjoyed Alec Baldwin in this film, and it kind of made me wonder what happened to Alec Baldwin a little bit.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:48.120
<v Mike>Because at this time in his life, he's doing so many big, big movies.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.320
<v Mike>Blockbusters is questionable on some of these things, but just prolific films.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.840
<v Mike>And I feel like we just, ever since he shot that person, we don't hear from him anymore.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:58.760
<v Mike>Well, I guess that makes sense.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.380
<v Sean>Yeah, well, you got to go there, huh?

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.860
<v Mike>Yeah, well, it happened to him, but I'd say even before that he dropped off.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.240
<v Mike>Maybe he dropped off when Cat in the Hat came out, but I would argue that was his peak.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:17.400
<v Sean>I mean, I know him most as, like, a comedic actor with 30 Rock, but I'm probably in the minority there.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.960
<v Joe>Well, I feel like that became such a part of his brand, is that, like,

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.200
<v Joe>so serious it's not serious kind of humor.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.860
<v Sean>Yeah. That's a great description of that.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.060
<v Mike>And I guess, you know, submarines are pretty cool themselves, right?

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:40.240
<v Mike>I think we covered that in our silent service episode, that submarine simulators,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:45.120
<v Mike>or just the idea of controlling a submarine as a unique vehicle, is pretty cool.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:51.820
<v Mike>And the Hunt for Red October submarine has an experimental caterpillar drive feature,

00:07:52.120 --> 00:08:00.080
<v Mike>which, pretty central to the plot, makes it so that it can basically become invisible underwater and not be detected.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.780
<v Mike>And that's something that's obviously going to fit into the game, too.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.220
<v Mike>But since you were talking about, Joe, watching it with your dad, maybe, perhaps.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.680
<v Mike>It's like, I do think that that's also, like, kind of how this audience fits in, too, though,

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:20.140
<v Mike>is that, like, if you were a kid, the movie was probably, like, cool, like, to look at,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.020
<v Mike>but you didn't understand really, like, what was going on,

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.820
<v Mike>or you were, like, maybe a little bored by, like, the dialogue stuff,

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.240
<v Mike>and you were like, ah, can't they just get back to the submarine footage?

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.300
<v Mike>So the game, rightfully, gives you, it's all submarine.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:34.080
<v Sean>It's, like, the idea is just...

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:34.920
<v Mike>Well, not the cutscenes.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:38.260
<v Joe>It's got those cutscenes that you feel like they're lifted right out of the movie.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:39.960
<v Joe>I think they might be ported from the movie.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.140
<v Mike>Like FMVs, but for NES.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.000
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. But the submarine is basically the maker break of the game,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.080
<v Mike>and the gameplay, it's very interesting.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.920
<v Mike>Obviously, on the NES, they're not going to try to, like,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:55.620
<v Mike>faithfully recreate the movie.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.040
<v Mike>How could they? I'm not even sure what that would look like.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.620
<v Mike>But instead of recreating a submarine simulator, even,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.780
<v Mike>the NES version turns into kind of a...

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:06.840
<v Sean>Oops, all shmup.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.700
<v Sean>Well, is it a shmup, though?

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.900
<v Sean>It's a shmup.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.000
<v Mike>I don't know.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.460
<v Mike>I don't know either, because, like, on paper, really,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.080
<v Joe>the only thing that differentiates it from a shmup

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.560
<v Joe>is that, like, you kind of control the camera.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:21.900
<v Mike>Even that's not fully true.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.520
<v Joe>Yeah. But that, like, small difference, you sort of do.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.540
<v Joe>It just moves really slow if you're not moving it,

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.320
<v Joe>but you decide to speed it up.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:34.280
<v Joe>Like, you're moving it forward faster than it's, like,

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.400
<v Joe>than it's regular, like, automatic movement.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:41.420
<v Sean>But, like, that little tiny difference does make it feel very different

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:42.300
<v Joe>than a shmup to me.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.760
<v Joe>It makes the movement feel different to me.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.100
<v Mike>I feel like it's ignoring a lot of the rules of shmups, too.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.240
<v Mike>Like, it's very...

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.940
<v Mike>Even with the other enemies, like, it's not some...

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:55.460
<v Mike>Not that it has to be a bullet hell or whatever,

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.260
<v Mike>but there's not really, like, a screen littered with enemies

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:59.180
<v Mike>where you're constantly clearing stuff.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.380
<v Mike>It is more like action navigation,

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:03.580
<v Mike>getting through these obstacles

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:10.180
<v Mike>and the ability to fire both head-on and above you.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:13.200
<v Mike>So there's definitely some things going on here

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.580
<v Mike>that could bring it up to discussion.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.200
<v Mike>But I wouldn't say calling it a shmup is unfair, Sean,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:18.560
<v Mike>because at the end of the day,

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.740
<v Mike>all you're doing is controlling this vehicle

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:25.360
<v Mike>like you would any other vehicle in these horizontal games

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.820
<v Mike>across from a beginning to end to get to a destination.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:34.620
<v Mike>And even, like, the places you play in the game,

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.780
<v Mike>it's just kind of like, where is this on Earth?

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:39.080
<v Sean>It's in the ocean.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.900
<v Mike>Yeah, but where do they have, like, giant walls

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.740
<v Mike>and retractable pipes and, like, where does that all exist?

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:46.620
<v Joe>I assume those were, like...

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.560
<v Joe>In the ocean. Yeah, that's just, like, on the beach

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.680
<v Joe>that you already see that stuff.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.060
<v Joe>You're not going to be, like, waist deep

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.700
<v Joe>and you're already seeing that stuff.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.420
<v Mike>And I don't know or pretend to know anything about submarines.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:10:59.520
<v Mike>Most of my submarine knowledge

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.260
<v Mike>comes from the aforementioned silent service.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.720
<v Mike>But is it really that easy to move a submarine in reverse?

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:09.180
<v Mike>Is it really that easy to just, like, back up a submarine

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.160
<v Mike>like it is in this game?

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.900
<v Sean>It's almost as if, like, they're calling it...

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.720
<v Sean>It's almost as if, like, the control scheme, for the most part,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.600
<v Sean>is sort of ported from shmups.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:21.180
<v Joe>See, all right, here's the thing, though.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.300
<v Joe>I guess, for me, just being able to come to a complete stop

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.960
<v Joe>feels not super shmup-like to me.

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:29.400
<v Sean>That's a very...

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.980
<v Sean>I think you guys have a very narrow description

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:33.620
<v Sean>of what shmups are.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.140
<v Joe>Well, all right, that's one of the elements.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.200
<v Sean>But also, like, a shmup, like Mike was saying, too,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.200
<v Joe>I feel like a shmup has, like, tons of targets on screen

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.300
<v Joe>where I'm, like, shooting them up.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.120
<v Joe>It feels like I need to be shooting a lot

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.080
<v Joe>at a lot of enemies.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.200
<v Joe>And there are portions of this game that are like that.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.920
<v Joe>But there are large portions of this game

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.360
<v Joe>where it's, like, you're quietly navigating

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.320
<v Joe>through, like, little rocks and everything.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:57.160
<v Mike>Yeah, you don't usually have a health bar in shmups, either.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.740
<v Joe>And I'm not saying that this definitely isn't a shmup.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:01.820
<v Joe>I'm just saying this does not feel like other shmups to me.

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:09.480
<v Sean>It's a very polished and with a few extra bells and whistles shmup.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:14.960
<v Sean>But we could have this conversation the whole episode if we want.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.300
<v Mike>Yeah, I think we should. I think we should continue to.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.200
<v Mike>In the favor of shmups, as I mentioned, with your primary weapons,

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.960
<v Mike>the A button firing torpedoes horizontally

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.680
<v Mike>and the B button firing missiles vertically

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.780
<v Mike>because you have enemies both above and to the side of you.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:33.140
<v Mike>So you have to deal with them.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:37.760
<v Mike>There are making a lot of good use of the NES controller, though,

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.080
<v Mike>because the select button is also what you use

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.320
<v Mike>to activate the Caterpillar drive we were talking about,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.060
<v Mike>which helps the Red October, which is—

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.040
<v Mike>I feel like we didn't mention this.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.040
<v Mike>The Red October is the name of the submarine.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:51.440
<v Mike>It's not what they're hunting for.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.160
<v Mike>Everybody else is hunting the Red October.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:59.520
<v Mike>And so you press the select button to kind of evade enemies.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.700
<v Mike>And then if you press A and B together,

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.760
<v Mike>you get basically your emergency defense system,

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:12.560
<v Mike>your decoys, electronic countermeasures,

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:13.620
<v Mike>I think they're called in the manual,

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.020
<v Mike>that attract and destroy nearby income enemy fire, too.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.760
<v Mike>So those are all neat features that I think

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.620
<v Mike>would actually welcome in a shmup, right?

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:24.800
<v Mike>A select button that kind of turns your vehicle temporarily invisible,

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.520
<v Mike>pressing the two buttons together

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:32.500
<v Mike>to temporarily send a decoy out that could take hits for you.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.020
